meta {
  name: Match — renamed detection (fuzzy)
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{mcp_confluence}}/match
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{mcp_confluence_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "alert_name": "Malware detected in download file"
  }
}

tests {
  test("200", function() { expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200); });
  test("matched, and admits it guessed", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().matched).to.equal(true);
    expect(res.getBody().how).to.equal("fuzzy");
    expect(res.getBody().similarity).to.be.above(0.85);
  });
}

docs {
  "download" against "downloaded" — one letter, 0.971.

  `how` is returned with every answer for this reason. A fuzzy match is a guess
  that cleared a threshold, and an analyst reading criteria written for a
  detection with a different name should be told that is what they are reading.
  The rendered playbook block says so to the model as well.

  Lower FUZZY_CUTOFF and this starts matching things it should not; raise it
  and renamed detections stop resolving. 0.85 is the notebook's figure.
}
